ucture bill could take until August to pass and risks not advancing at all if lawmakers cannot pass it before the midterms. Greg Cipolaro, head of research at NYDIG, said the July 4 target mentioned earlier by Patrick Witt, a senior White House crypto adviser, looked more like an aspirational benchmark than a fixed legislative deadline. Cipolaro said the realistic window was June through early August. The article explains that the bill is one of the most important pieces of crypto legislation this year because it would outline how US watchdogs regulate crypto, but it has been delayed by disputes over stablecoins, ethics, government officials’ use of crypto, and decentralized finance enforcement. It notes that the Senate Banking Committee passed a long-delayed markup on Thursday and moved the bill to the Senate floor, where it will need 60 votes to avoid prolonged debate. Republicans hold a 53-seat Senate majority, so the bill would need support from at least seven Democrats. Some Democrats argue the bill does not go far enough in preventing crime and sanctions evasion. Cipolaro warned that Congress then faces a narrowing calendar: a recess from late July to early September, then a period leading into the November midterm elections when Senate leadership is unlikely to schedule a contested 60-vote floor fight. If the bill misses that window, he said the most likely remaining path is a post-election lame-duck session, and even that depends on Republicans retaining the Senate and Majority Leader John Thune prioritizing the measure over government funding deadlines. He also said that if Democrats gain control of the Senate, the current Republican-backed version is unlikely to advance in the next Congress beginning in January. The article adds that if the bill is passed and signed into law, crypto markets would likely benefit because major institutions would gain enough confidence to invest in the sector thanks to clearer rules. Cipolaro said the bill would also provide regulatory certainty to Bitcoin by classifying it as a commodity under the Commodity Futures Trading Commission and closing what he called the last significant regulatory overhang for Bitcoin as an institutional asset class. But he also warned the bill could still fail because negotiations could stall over ethics or decentralized finance enforcement language, or because scheduling delays could prevent action. CoinDesk’s RSS item for the same news cycle, titled “The Clarity Act took a step forward: State of Crypto,” described the issue as unpacking Thursday’s at-times contentious markup hearing. That framing matches Cointelegraph’s account of the Senate Banking Committee advancing the bill, but it emphasizes that the legislative move was only a step forward rather than final passage. Taken together, the two sources show a single dominant story: Congress advanced the crypto market-structure bill, but the path to law remains narrow, politically fragile, and highly sensitive to the Senate calendar. The shared theme is not just that the bill moved forward, but that the next several weeks may determine whether crypto regulation gets a breakthrough in 2026 or slips into a later political window. The market implication is important because the bill could give Bitcoin clearer treatment under federal law, while also opening the door to broader institutional participation if lawmakers can resolve the remaining disputes. The tone across both sources is cautious: progress is real, but the deadline pressure is increasing. Cointelegraph also noted that the industry is still operating under permanent jurisdictional ambiguity if the legislation stalls, which helps explain why the story matters beyond Washington. The bill is being watched as a signal for whether lawmakers can create a workable framework for the digital asset market, especially at a moment when both policy and market structure debates are moving quickly. The recurring facts across the feeds are the markup passage, the need for 60 votes, the seven-Democrat hurdle, the June-to-early-August window, the midterm risk, and the potential Bitcoin commodity classification. Those are the core details that define the event.\",\n  \"CHART_COIN\": \"bitcoin\"\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779087240682}}
